1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
Thank you.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,300
How are you guys this morning?

3
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:42,880
I would like to start this morning, if we can, by everybody standing together.

4
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:43,980
I know you just sat down.

5
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:50,600
But if we could all stand together, I'd like us to recite the Lord's Prayer together, okay?

6
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:53,020
We're going to put it up on the screen for those.

7
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:56,980
This is when the disciples asked Jesus, how is it that we should pray?

8
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:01,000
Jesus instructed them in Matthew 6 to do it this way.

9
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,780
So together, our Father who is in heaven, hallowed be your name.

10
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:10,920
Your kingdom come and your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.

11
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:19,360
Give us this day our daily bread and forgive us our trespasses as we have forgiven those who trespassed against us.

12
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:23,420
Do not lead us into temptation, but deliver us from evil.

13
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:27,360
For yours is the kingdom and the power, glory forever.

14
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:28,060
Amen.

15
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:28,840
Thank you.

16
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:29,540
You may be seated.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,280
So in that second line, third line on this slide, we're instructed by Jesus to pray to God that his kingdom should come.

18
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:42,300
And that his will be done on earth, right?

19
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:43,980
Just like it is in heaven.

20
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:45,280
And we know everything's perfect in heaven.

21
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:46,360
On earth it doesn't feel that way.

22
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:48,760
But we're praying that his kingdom come, his will be done.

23
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:54,960
That's the same directive that Christ gave at the end of Matthew, the beginning of Acts, that we call the Great Commission.

24
00:01:55,120 --> 00:02:04,460
I'm telling you to go out into all the world and preach the gospel to all nations, baptizing men in the name of the Father, God, and the Son, and the Holy Ghost, making disciples, right?

25
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:07,480
That's the mission that he sent us out on.

26
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:10,600
We're in a series on Paul.

27
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:15,980
And we've spent the first part of this series not talking about Paul's teaching, but talking about his life.

28
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:20,620
Because his life, he was an exemplar of a life driven by mission.

29
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:21,260
What mission?

30
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:22,740
That mission, right?

31
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:36,380
Paul had come to recognize that the risen Messiah was real, and he was spreading that to all people so that that Messiah's kingdom could be built on earth.

32
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:41,260
Today, we're going to talk about the very end of Acts, which is the end of Paul's life.

33
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:44,100
It's the last eight chapters, so clearly we're not going to be in great detail.

34
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:45,160
Eight chapters to cover.

35
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,420
Chapters 21 to 28.

36
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:52,140
And in that, we have Paul's fourth, is it a missionary journey?

37
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:53,820
Is it a prison journey?

38
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:54,920
People have called it different things.

39
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:56,040
Paul didn't call it anything.

40
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:57,380
He called it Thursday, right?

41
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:58,280
And then Friday and Saturday.

42
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:06,640
But it was a journey that he went on during which, in this eight chapters, he is beaten.

43
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:07,780
He's thrown out of the temple.

44
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:08,820
He's falsely accused.

45
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:10,340
There's an assassination plot.

46
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,360
He's imprisoned for two years.

47
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:15,020
He goes through all these seemingly bogus trials.

48
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:23,040
There's this long, difficult winter journey on the Mediterranean that ends in a two-week, being lost in a storm for two weeks.

49
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:24,280
He's shipwrecked on an island.

50
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:25,500
A viper bites him.

51
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:26,920
He lives through it.

52
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:30,600
He's accused of being first a criminal and then a God.

53
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:32,260
And finally, they listen to his story.

54
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:35,320
And finally, there's another difficult journey.

55
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:41,160
And finally, in the very end of Acts, he makes it to the peninsula of Italy to head to Rome.

56
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:52,280
And when he gets there, the book of Acts culminates in what he sort of discovers through this whole trip that's described through the book of Acts.

57
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:57,720
We know about what happened with Paul in Acts because he's traveling with a guy named Luke.

58
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,180
Luke was a doctor, okay?

59
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:00,980
Paul traveled with a doctor.

60
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:03,620
And Luke was also a Roman.

61
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:13,180
And he documented everything very carefully because, as an outsider, he is documenting all these things that sound incredible in great detail.

62
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:23,400
In fact, modern non-religious historians have studied Luke's travel log that's in Acts as a great source of information on ancient travel.

63
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:28,480
He was that detailed because he wanted people to understand this really happened in your world, right?

64
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:32,680
I'm telling you a normal thing, normal thing, incredible thing, but it all really happened, right?

65
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:34,940
And I want you to know that I've researched and I'm telling you the truth.

66
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:45,760
So Luke tells us the story of Acts, of Christ in the Gospel of Luke and the story of the early church and then Paul in the book of Acts as like a two-scroll set.

67
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:48,580
And it's written to a man named he calls Theophilus.

68
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:50,360
Theophilus is a Greek name.

69
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:51,680
This was a Gentile.

70
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:53,480
And it means lover of God.

71
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:59,360
So this is a Gentile who was a Gentile who'd come to believe in the one true God, right?

72
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:01,340
It may have been his name.

73
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:04,440
It was probably an alias, a nickname.

74
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:11,960
But there was a class of person back in Paul's day and well before Paul, back into the Old Testament, called God-fearers.

75
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:14,860
They would be Gentiles who'd come to believe in the one true God.

76
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:21,560
And they're interested in it and they fear God, but they don't go the whole nine yards because they don't want to be circumcised as adults.

77
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:23,700
Not a popular move.

78
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:29,760
And they don't necessarily want to ethnically and nationally identify as Jewish, even though they believe in the Jewish religion.

79
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:35,240
At the time, there was a very fuzzy line between being ethnically Jewish and religiously Jewish.

80
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:39,840
And the God-fearing Gentiles sort of stayed on one side of that fuzzy line, right?

81
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:48,760
But now Paul has come and he's attracting them to the church with an expansion on the understanding of how that gospel works.

82
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:51,420
And that's what Luke records for us in Acts.

83
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:57,200
In Acts, we're told about the beginnings of the church and then how a persecution broke out.

84
00:05:57,580 --> 00:05:59,640
And the persecution was led by Paul.

85
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:01,060
We've talked about that in previous weeks.

86
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:02,200
He was named Saul at the time.

87
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:14,340
And he was so zealous of the Old Testament law and his dedication to God that he was very upset about those who were misinterpreting and misrepresenting the gospel.

88
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:17,260
Or what he didn't call the gospel, but the Old Testament law.

89
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:30,460
His view was that we have to follow the law zealously so that the circumstances can be right, so that the Messiah can come, so that the Gentiles can be overthrown, our oppressors can be destroyed.

90
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:34,420
And then we will become the great nation that God set us up to be.

91
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:39,840
He was named after Saul, the first warrior king of a warrior tribe.

92
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:43,200
It was a name with national dignity and pride in it, right?

93
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:44,240
That's who Saul was.

94
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:53,780
And so when he persecuted the church, it was in great zealousness for the law and for the following God and for the hope of the Messiah to come.

95
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:57,340
And then one day on the roads of Damascus, Paul met the Messiah.

96
00:06:57,340 --> 00:06:59,760
The resurrected Christ came to Paul.

97
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:03,600
And it was a life-changing moment, which we often refer to as Paul's conversion.

98
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:05,080
But that is probably misleading.

99
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:08,780
It wasn't a moment of conversion from Jewishness to Christianity.

100
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:10,520
Christianity didn't really exist yet.

101
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:11,620
They weren't called Christians yet.

102
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:14,780
It was a moment of elation.

103
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:16,560
Everything he'd wanted just happened.

104
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:19,640
He'd been working forever his whole life to find a way to get the Messiah here.

105
00:07:19,640 --> 00:07:21,020
And the Messiah was revealed to him.

106
00:07:21,020 --> 00:07:24,820
It was a moment of epiphany and revelation.

107
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:25,780
And he was thrilled.

108
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:29,140
And he spent the rest of his adult life going to his Jewish brother saying,

109
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:30,840
the Messiah has actually come.

110
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:38,520
And what we understood to be the law has actually now been fulfilled through the coming of the Messiah and made obsolete.

111
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:42,240
And you can ask yourself, how can something be fulfilled and made obsolete at the same time?

112
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:48,540
And I would say to you, in the same way that a man with a crooked back has a crutch whose purpose is to hold him aright and help him move,

113
00:07:48,540 --> 00:07:55,080
when he meets the healer that heals his back, fulfilling the purpose of the crutch so that he can now stand direct and move,

114
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:57,800
the crutch is rendered obsolete when its purpose is fulfilled.

115
00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:58,860
Does that make sense?

116
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:00,380
That's what the law was.

117
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:02,260
And that's what Paul is saying to his brothers.

118
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:04,400
There is a Messiah who's come.

119
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:09,820
The separation between man and God is gone because he's created a path back to God.

120
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:12,280
There can be, when you feel the weight of your sin on your shoulders,

121
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:16,840
you don't have to go in guilt and try to propitiate before God with sacrifice.

122
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:18,960
He has made the final sacrifice for you.

123
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:20,560
He has become the Messiah.

124
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:21,760
There's a path back to God.

125
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:27,080
And you can now, without going through all the rights and laws and specific rules,

126
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:30,020
be close to God because he's come down to touch us.

127
00:08:30,020 --> 00:08:31,960
That's the gospel, right?

128
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:34,340
And Paul went and said that over and over and over.

129
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:38,100
And the result was the Jews that he was going to and saying this,

130
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:40,540
what they heard was that everything that they'd been defending,

131
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:43,900
everything they'd been working toward was now being called less important.

132
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:49,080
And Paul was rejoicing because all the Gentiles were coming to the faith.

133
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:52,520
All these God-fearers who now didn't have to be circumcised to be part of the church,

134
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:53,580
they were thrilled.

135
00:08:53,580 --> 00:08:55,440
They were coming in droves, right?

136
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:57,160
It was very, very exciting.

137
00:08:57,160 --> 00:09:03,100
There were many, many reasons why this was far more appealing than the pagan fates that they were relegated to.

138
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:06,540
And they were now able to overcome that divide.

139
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:08,420
And Paul rejoiced in this.

140
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:14,120
And every time Paul rejoiced in the well-being of a Gentile,

141
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:17,360
that really hurt his brethren that he'd been going out to speak to, right?

142
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:22,820
Because they prayed daily that the Gentiles would be overthrown and the Romans destroyed and the Messiah come.

143
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:25,020
And Paul's like, no, when the Messiah came, it didn't work that way.

144
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:25,940
It's not just for the Jews.

145
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:27,480
It's for everybody, right?

146
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:29,820
So that's the gospel that Paul's preaching.

147
00:09:29,820 --> 00:09:35,780
And he's not, what should be a moment of joy is actually driving a wedge between him and his countrymen, right?

148
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:43,720
And he eventually is called Paul because that's his Greek name, right?

149
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:46,500
When he's speaking more and more in Greek to more and more Gentiles,

150
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:53,240
and he drops the pretense of being a follower, the scion of an ancient king of Israel,

151
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:56,380
and says, hey man, I'm just here talking to you in your language, I'm Paul.

152
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:58,840
And that drives the wedge further.

153
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:05,680
And all of this comes to a head at the beginning of his fourth missionary journey,

154
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:07,460
which is what we're here to talk about tonight, right?

155
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:10,960
At the time that Corey talked about Barnabas several weeks back,

156
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:16,640
Barnabas was a guy who identified that Paul was unique and that he was a Roman citizen and a Jewish scholar.

157
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:21,600
So then was uniquely qualified to carry this word to the wider Roman empire.

158
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:25,300
And at the same time that happened, there was a famine in the land.

159
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:27,940
And of course, Paul had been part of a persecution in the land.

160
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:31,260
And so the people in Judea were not doing well.

161
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:35,700
And so Paul and Barnabas said, when we go out and spread the gospel to, and the other apostles,

162
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:39,220
they said, when we go out and spread the gospel to the other edges of the empire,

163
00:10:39,220 --> 00:10:45,080
we're going to ask every time that they give some of these are the wealthy and privileged cities outside of Judea.

164
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:49,860
So we're going to ask that they give some wealth that we can bring back and support their brothers that are being persecuted back in Judea.

165
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:54,400
That was in Acts 10, I think, maybe Acts 11, 11, 29.

166
00:10:54,400 --> 00:11:00,880
And so throughout the three missionary journeys that Ryan talked about through all the letters of Paul, you hear him fundraising.

167
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:05,840
And if you've ever wondered why he's fundraising and why people are giving money, it's for this mission,

168
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:12,360
this fundraising effort to support the churches that are being persecuted that Paul persecuted back in Judea.

169
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:23,300
So at the end of his third missionary journey, Paul has gathered all these funds and he's traveling back to Jerusalem to give money to the persecuted churches and say,

170
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:28,720
hey, your fear that we're against you, that wedge that you feel between us, that's not real, right?

171
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:31,460
We've all been gathering money to take care of you.

172
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:32,720
We want to unify the church.

173
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:33,480
We're in this together.

174
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:35,060
That's Paul's vision.

175
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:48,500
In Acts 21, when his fourth journey begins, it's because when he gets to Jerusalem, people who have heard him telling the new understanding of the Old Testament,

176
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:56,700
that the niggling rules of the law are no longer essential, that God has come for the Gentiles as well, and are very upset about it.

177
00:11:56,700 --> 00:11:59,040
They're in Jerusalem too, and they see him there.

178
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,860
And he's traveling with Luke, a Gentile doctor, right?

179
00:12:01,860 --> 00:12:03,320
And other Gentiles.

180
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,980
And they don't really wait to see, is he really a dedicated Jew?

181
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:10,340
They don't wait to hear the details.

182
00:12:10,340 --> 00:12:19,200
When they see him in the temple doing his very devout rites, because he is a loyal Jew, when they see him in here, they just scream out,

183
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:21,840
men of Israel, come to our aid in Acts 21, 28.

184
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:26,860
And they drag him bodily and throw him back out into the court of the Gentiles, where they think he belongs.

185
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:32,560
They seal off the temple so it can't be defiled by his presence.

186
00:12:32,660 --> 00:12:38,160
And they just start beating him to a pulp in the court of the Gentiles, which is the area outside.

187
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:43,840
And the Romans, who metaphorically and physically oversee the Jewish population in the temple,

188
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:48,560
they see a huge disturbance of violence going on, and the soldiers rush down, separate the crowd,

189
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:51,460
and pull the guy out of the middle that seems to be the cause of the disturbance.

190
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:55,620
It says they literally had to hold him over their shoulders to get him out of there, take him up the steps.

191
00:12:55,660 --> 00:12:58,780
And Paul says, no, no, no, wait, this has been a huge misunderstanding.

192
00:12:58,780 --> 00:13:00,780
I don't want to press charges or anything.

193
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:05,300
I just, can you put me down and let me explain to these people what they've misunderstood, right?

194
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:08,260
And he turns around and he starts telling them the gospel.

195
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:11,500
And the Jews are receptive, because they believe in this God.

196
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:13,260
They want to hear if the Messiah has come.

197
00:13:13,260 --> 00:13:14,220
They're kind of interested.

198
00:13:14,340 --> 00:13:18,960
They've heard, it's been 20 years since Christ was in Jerusalem, and he was well lauded.

199
00:13:18,960 --> 00:13:21,880
So this story has been around, and they want to hear more about it.

200
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:22,700
And they listen.

201
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:26,040
As he speaks in Aramaic, the Roman soldiers don't really know what he's saying,

202
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:27,220
because he's speaking in the same language.

203
00:13:27,220 --> 00:13:32,460
And he gets to the point where he says, and so the gospel is now available to the Gentiles.

204
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:33,640
That's about as far as he gets, right?

205
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:41,600
They erupt again, chaos ensues, and he's never really able to speak to the Jewish population again, right?

206
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:49,600
At this point in the story, Luke starts drawing some really strong parallels between Christ's experience in the book of Luke

207
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:52,040
and Paul's experience in the book of Acts.

208
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:58,480
Both are falsely accused, and both are rushed to judgment by mob and sensed with religious fervor.

209
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:04,640
Both times, that's because they were spreading this gospel, that God is here for the forgiveness of sins,

210
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:05,700
and all men can come to him.

211
00:14:05,700 --> 00:14:12,820
They're both taken for judgment by the Romans, and then allowed to stand in trial before the Jewish religious court.

212
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:15,960
And both times, those trials go very badly.

213
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:19,360
There's unreliable witnesses brought forth, and it kind of descends into chaos.

214
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:25,060
In both cases, they're sent to be scourged by the Romans as sort of a blunt inducement

215
00:14:25,060 --> 00:14:27,480
against ever doing anything to annoy the Romans again, right?

216
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:28,620
Just stop it.

217
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:34,040
In the case of Paul, at that moment, he says, oh wait, I'm a Roman citizen.

218
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:36,460
You can't scourge me without a trial.

219
00:14:36,460 --> 00:14:44,280
But from that scourging, Christ and Paul both are now on their path to execution.

